{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789426539,"nonce":"fYb94/2gN7g5GF17x2oJuqyU83g=","title":"rt seam: a host's collector needs the thread transitions Thread and Isolate make","message":"A host that serves the memory slots (caribou fills rt with its Immix heap, one heap for several languages) has a stop-the-world of its own, over every OS thread that touches that heap. Since 6771870 and f801d0e a program's worker and isolate threads touch it too, and the seam tells the host nothing about them, so the host's collector cannot bring them to rest and the two worlds deadlock.\n\nWhat happens today under caribou (caribou-wren fills rt):\n\n- Thread: eight `Thread.create` tasks allocating lists. The host's `alloc_plain` records each allocation for the heap (its pin list, kept per VM as the seam implied one thread per heap); two workers allocate at once and the list's reallocation corrupts malloc (`BUG IN LIBMALLOC ... mfm_free`, exit 133). The same program on the built-in heap prints 301688.\n- Isolate: four isolates summing 200000 numbers, the main thread on `reply.receive()`. Each isolate's cycle ends in the host's collection, which waits for every thread it knows to reach one of its safepoints; the main thread is parked in `Sched::idle` (a condvar wait inside `channel_receive` → `park_on` → `drive_until`), which the host cannot see, so after 2 s it gives up (`gave up stopping the world after 2013ms; 1 of 5 mutators never reached a safepoint`) and the program wedges. The isolate threads themselves allocate through `heap_new` on their own thread, so the host learns of them; the worker pool's threads it never learns of.\n\n`stw.rs` already has exactly what the host needs: a thread's record moves between RUNNING and SAFE at `enter_safe`/`leave_safe`, publishing its stack pointer, and a safe thread is one the collector scans where it stands. Ash's seam exposes the same three facts (`register_thread`, `gc_set_blocking(true/false)` around a blocking region, `gc_safepoint`), which is how the host's collector stops Ash's threads. Proposed slots, defaults doing nothing:\n\n- `thread_start()` / `thread_stop()`: a worker or isolate thread is about to run Wren on this heap, and is done. The host registers it as a mutator and forgets it.\n- `thread_safe(sp: usize)` / `thread_running()`: the transitions `enter_safe`/`leave_safe` make. While safe, the host's collector need not wait for the thread and scans its stack from `sp`; the host treats it as a blocking region.\n- `thread_poll()`: from the safepoint page's slow path and the interpreter's poll, so a stop the host requested is honoured by a compiled loop that allocates nothing. (Today caribou's `should_collect` parks when the host asked, but the compiled loop header since e61b255 loads from the page and never asks.)\n\nWith those the host can run its collection while wren_lift's world has the threads safe, and wren_lift's cycle can run while the host's is idle; each side's request parks the other's threads at the places they already stop.\n\nAlso since 249c239: the host reaches the program's engine and interner through the thread's view now (`vm.request_tier_up(id)`), fine; but `ASH_GC_STRESS=1` and `WLIFT_GC_STRESS=1` crash caribou's interop tests since that commit, and since e61b255 the heap's heartbeat (a busy loop that never allocates) no longer runs a cycle: the loop header polls the page, not `should_collect`. Both are tracked on caribou's side as 16d8592; the poll slot above would cover the second.","files":null}]}